er to use only one degree of freedom,  even though the equation governing it has 3 terms. For 10 points each:\", \"answer\": \"\"}, {\"label\": \"A\", \"question\": \"Name this idealized model of a population, which assumes no migration, no genetic drift, no assortative mating,  and an infinite population size.\", \"answer\": \"Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium\"}, {\"label\": \"B\", \"question\": \"The 2-p-q term in the Hardy-Weinberg model gives the frequency of this kind of genotype. This kind of  genotype has two different alleles at one locus, and is usually written using a capital and a lowercase letter.\", \"answer\": \"heterozygous [or heterozygote or heterozygotic]\"}, {\"label\": \"C\", \"question\": \"A classic example of heterozygote advantage protects against malaria, which is transmitted by Plasmodium in  these blood-sucking insects. The name of these insects means “little fly.”\", \"answer\": \"mosquitos [accept Culicidae]\"}]}]}}",
