            "text": "{\n  \"study_topic_id\": \"study-1311dcbba49f8dbdb6ca\",\n  \"overview\": {\n    \"text\": \"Mosquitoes are slender, winged insects in the family Culicidae, with thousands of species; the name comes from Spanish for “little fly.” Their life cycle has four stages—egg, aquatic larva, aquatic pupa, and flying adult. In species that take blood meals, females feed on people or other animals because blood supplies protein for making eggs; males do not bite. Mosquitoes matter because some species carry pathogens between hosts, but the risk is species- and pathogen-specific. Malaria, for example, is transmitted by infected female Anopheles mosquitoes, and only a minority of Anopheles species can transmit it.\",\n    \"source_urls\": [\n      \"https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/8/ac-2308_article\",\n      \"https://www.cdc.gov/mosquitoes/about/life-cycle-of-aedes-mosquitoes.html\",\n      \"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\"\n    ]\n  },\n  \"key_facts\": [\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Mosquitoes belong to the family Culicidae; the word “mosquito” comes from Spanish for “little fly.”\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/8/ac-2308_article\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Mosquitoes develop through four stages: egg, larva, pupa, and adult; the first three stages are aquatic.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"In blood-feeding mosquito species, females use blood to obtain protein for egg production; males do not bite.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/8/ac-2308_article\",\n        \"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\"\n      ]\n    },\n    {\n      \"text\": \"Only female Anopheles mosquitoes transmit human malaria, and only a minority of Anopheles species transmit it in nature.\",\n      \"source_urls\": [\n        \"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\"\n      ]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"sources\": [\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Hematophagous Endeavors, Fact and Fancy\",\n      \"url\": \"https://wwwnc.cdc.gov/eid/article/23/8/ac-2308_article\",\n      \"publisher\": \"Emerging Infectious Diseases, Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24T18:47:28.659131+00:00\",\n      \"supports\": \"Family Culicidae, Spanish name origin, blood-feeding females, egg production, and the fact that some mosquitoes transmit disease-causing agents.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"Life Cycle of Aedes Mosquitoes\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cdc.gov/mosquitoes/about/life-cycle-of-aedes-mosquitoes.html\",\n      \"publisher\": \"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24T18:47:28.659131+00:00\",\n      \"supports\": \"The egg, larva, pupa, and adult stages; aquatic immature stages; and female Aedes blood-feeding for egg production.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"title\": \"CDC - DPDx - Malaria\",\n      \"url\": \"https://www.cdc.gov/dpdx/malaria/\",\n      \"publisher\": \"Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\",\n      \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-09-24T18:47:28.659131+00:00\",\n      \"supports\": \"Mosquito life stages, male and female feeding differences, and malaria transmission by female Anopheles mosquitoes, with only a subset of Anopheles species acting as vectors.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"research_queries\": [\n    \"site:cdc.gov mosquitoes life cycle females blood feeding Culicidae\",\n    \"site:who.int mosquito malaria female Anopheles transmit malaria\",\n    \"site:si.edu mosquito biology Culicidae proboscis life cycle\"\n  ],\n  \"notes\": \"The overview is one 91-word paragraph. Each overview claim and key fact is linked to opened source pages. General mosquito traits are distinguished from traits described specifically for Aedes or Anopheles.\",\n  \"unresolved_concerns\": [],\n  \"self_check_complete\": true\n}"
